<div id="pf72" class="pf w0 h0" data-page-no="72"><div class="pc pc72 w0 h0"><img class="bi x0 y0 w1 h1" alt="" src="data:image/png;base64,"/><div class="c x1 y65 w2 hb"><div class="t m0 x43 he ya0d ff13 fs3 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">Figur<span class="_ _0"></span>e 3-1. Matt T<span class="_ _0"></span>ur<span class="_ _1"></span>ck’<span class="_ _6"></span>s MAD data landscape</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya0e ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">Even relatively successful technologies often fade into obscurity quickly</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya0f ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">after a few years of rapid adoption, a victim of their success. For instance,</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya10 ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">Hive was met with rapid uptake because it allowed both analysts and</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya11 ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">engineers to query massive data sets without coding complex MapReduce</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya12 ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">jobs by hand. Inspired by the success of Hive, but wishing to improve on its</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya13 ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">shortcomings, engineers developed SparkSQL, Presto, and other</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya14 ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">technologies. Hive now appears primarily in legacy deployments. This is</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya15 ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">the general cycle of data technologies—incumbents reign for a time, then</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya16 ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">get disrupted and replaced by new technologies that also reign for a time,</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya17 ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">and so on.</div><div class="t m0 x6 hc ya18 ff11 fs4 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">Our Advice</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya19 ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">Given the rapid pace of tooling and best practice changes, we suggest</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya1a ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">evaluating tools on a two-year basis (<span class="fc2">Figure 3-2</span>). Whenever possible, find</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya1b ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">the immutable technologies along the data engineering lifecycle, and use</div><div class="t m0 x6 h5 ya1c ff12 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">those as your base. Build transitory tools around the immutables.</div><div class="t m0 x21 he ya1d ff13 fs3 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws0">Figur<span class="_ _0"></span>e 3-2. Use a two-year time horizon to reevaluate your technology choices</div></div><a class="l" href="#pf72" data-dest-detail='[114,"XYZ",76.5,365.25,null]'><div class="d m1" style="border-style:none;position:absolute;left:491.421618px;bottom:677.696078px;width:63.000000px;height:16.500000px;background-color:rgba(255,255,255,0.000001);"></div></a></div><div class="pi" data-data='{"ctm":[1.633987,0.000000,0.000000,1.633987,0.000000,0.000000]}'></div></div>
